  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 118 PM CDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nFog and low stratus have lifted across the area through this\nmorning. Satellite and surface observations at 18z show a weak\ncyclone right over the forecast area. This weak circulation is\nleading to warming temperatures across south-central Kansas and\ncooler temperatures and remaining cloud cover across the west.\nThis differential heating boundary from west to east across the\narea could serve as a focus for isolated thunderstorm\ndevelopment in south-central Kansas late this afternoon and\nearly evening. Only slight chance POPs were added to highlight\nthis risk. Lacking mid to upper level support should prevent\nstorm organization, so severe weather is not anticipated.\nHowever, cannot rule out a stronger storm or two with a strong\nwind gust potential given increasing DCAPE and LLLRs. Any storms\nwill diminish into the evening hours with loss of daytime\nheating.\n\nThe next potential for widespread rainfall will come on Friday\nwhen a weak storm system south of the area advects moisture\nnorthward. Light rain would be the expectation for the forecast\narea. NBM POPs are up around 70-90+% and while the intensity\nwould be low, seems reasonable given strong consensus in the\nguidance. Rainfall totals of 0.20-0.75\" are in the forecast, \nwith higher end of that range in southern counties, and lower \ntotals to the north and west counties.\n\nThe bigger storm system is still progged to move into the\nPlains Sunday and into the start of next week. Global \ndeterministic models and their ensembles continue to show a \ndeepening trough emerging across the Western U.S late this\nweekend. There appears to be two components to this system, a\nsouthern wave and a northern wave. How these components interact\nwill determine how impacts evolve for our area. While\nuncertainty still remains the evolution of the system and/or \nsystems, globals seem to be on board with a split surface low\npattern. One system develops and tracks through the northern\nPlains, and the other develops and tracks south of the forecast\narea. If this solution were to occur, this would put us in an\ninbetween phase which will complicate how much rainfall occurs\nand overall impacts to the area. Nevertheless, an unsettled \npattern, with increasing POPs and gusty winds are in the cards \nSunday through Tuesday morning.",
